2.2.13.m:
For him over whose sānnāyya the moon rises [early], he should divide the [grains] fit for the sacrificial cake into three parts.
Those that are medium-sized, he should offer [as a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni the Giver (Dātṛ).
Those that are largest, [as] a caru in sour milk to Indra the Bestower (Pradātṛ).
Those that are smallest, [as] a caru in boiled milk to Viṣṇu Śipiviṣṭa.
Agni is the giver of the medium;
Indra is the bestower of the greatest;
and that which is smallest, that is Śipiviṣṭa;
he attains that—
cattle indeed.
Soma is left over for him over whose sānnāyya the moon rises;
he is left over for cattle.
One who desires cattle, having sacrificed at the new moon, should drive away the calves.
He should divide the [grains] fit for the sacrificial cake into three parts.
Those that are smallest, he should offer [as a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Possessed of Gifts (Sanimant).
Those that are medium-sized, [as] a caru in boiled milk to Viṣṇu Śipiviṣṭa.
Those that are largest, [as] a caru in sour milk to Indra the Bestower.
Agni finds for him that which is here;
Viṣṇu, that which is in the intermediate space;
Indra, that which is in heaven.
Warriors seek cattle;
in that these grains of rice are divided,
these warriors are the seekers,
they are the blockers.
One who has attained fortune should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni;
a milch cow is its sacrificial fee.
Having moved eastwards, [he offers a cake] on three potsherds to Viṣṇu;
a milch mare is its sacrificial fee.
Having moved eastwards, having gone to a mountain or waters, [he offers] a caru in ghee to Prajāpati;
a female slave is its sacrificial fee.
In that there is [a cake] to Agni, he strides onto this [earth] by it;
in that there is one to Viṣṇu, onto the intermediate space by it;
in that there is one to Prajāpati, onto that world by it.
In that he moves eastwards, he steps onto [them];
in that he goes to a mountain or waters, he reaches the end.
In that there are three milch animals as sacrificial fee—
these worlds are three;
he makes these worlds milch cows for him;
he makes these worlds yield to him.
These worlds, having yielded, milk for him who knows thus.
For one who vomits Soma, he should offer a caru of millet to Soma and Indra (Somā-Indra).
Indra drank the Soma of Tvaṣṭṛ uninvited;
his upward drink of Soma fell down;
those became millets.
He who vomits Soma is deprived of the drink of Soma.
In that it is to Soma,
he unites him with the drink of Soma.
He who vomits Soma is deprived of indriyá-power and virility.
In that it is to Indra,
he unites him with indriyá-power and virility.
For the drink of Soma in him has become loose, as it were;
then he vomits Soma.
In that he mixes it with millet grains,
[it is] for the retention of the drink of Soma.

2.3.1.m:
One should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, [and] a curds-mixture to Mitra and Varuṇa;
he should make a diseased person sacrifice.
The [cake] to Agni is for success;
also, having made him possessed of bones, he establishes him.
The curds-mixture, indeed, if unappeased without an animal [sacrifice], is liable to wipe [the sacrificer] away.
An animal also should be seized,
for appeasement, that there may be no wiping away.
These are animals, namely rice and barley;
in that the sacrificial cake is made of rice,
by that alone the animal is seized,
for appeasement, that there may be no wiping away.
For a brahmin, it should be to Mitra and Varuṇa;
for the brahmin is of the nature of Mitra and Varuṇa by divinity.
For a rājanya, it should be to Indra and Varuṇa;
for the rājanya is of the nature of Indra and Varuṇa by divinity.
For a vaiśya, it should be to Agni and Varuṇa;
Agni is all the deities;
here is the divinity of the vaiśya also.
He should make a diseased person sacrifice;
he has approached his own deity with an expiation.
Seized by Varuṇa indeed is he who is diseased;
from Varuṇa he thereby releases him.
Man is milk; it is the milk of this [man] that is sick;
by means of milk he buys back his milk.
When he pushes apart,
for cutting open, they extract the arrow-point from the center;
thereby it is the consumption that they extract from his center.
Then, when having swept [it] together again he cuts off portions together for Agni, just as having extracted the arrow-point they bind it with a turban, so is that. He should make one desiring prosperity sacrifice;
he has approached his own deity with an expiation.
Seized by Varuṇa indeed is he who desires prosperity;
from Varuṇa he thereby releases him.
Man is milk;
he who desires prosperity desires milk;
by means of milk he secures milk for him.
When he pushes apart,
[it is] for the protection of the sacrifice.
Then, when he sweeps together again,
with prosperity he sweeps him together.
He should make one desiring a village sacrifice;
he has approached his own deity with an expiation.
Seized by Varuṇa indeed is he who desires a village;
from Varuṇa he thereby releases him.
Man is milk;
he who desires a village desires milk;
by means of milk he secures milk for him.
When he pushes apart,
[it is] for the protection of the sacrifice.
Then, when he sweeps together again,
with a village he sweeps him together.
He offers [cakes] on one potsherd;
here and there indeed are Varuṇa's nooses;
from them he releases him.
If he were to offer separately, that would be a dismemberment of the sacrifice.
All are to be offered in the fire,
for success.
'That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is full of vigor, with that we would worship you;
with that do release this so-and-so from distress!
That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is full of strength, with that we would worship you;
with that do release this so-and-so from distress!
That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is sorcerous, with that we would worship you;
with that do release this so-and-so from distress!
That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is demonic, with that we would worship you;
with that do release this so-and-so from distress!
That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is full of vigor, full of strength, sorcerous, demonic, with that we have worshipped you;
with that have released this so-and-so from distress!
That noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, which is among the gods, that of yours I avert with this [offering];
to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
That noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, which is in food, that of yours I avert with this [offering];
to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
That noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, which is among two-footed [and] four-footed cattle, that of yours I avert with this [offering];
to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
That noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, which is in plants, in trees, in the waters, on the earth, in the directions, that of yours I avert with this [offering];
to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!'
These indeed are Varuṇa's nooses;
from them he releases him.

2.3.2.m:
One who has rivals should offer a caru to the Viśve Devas.
The gods and the Asuras were contending.
Those gods sacrificed with the Saṃgrahaṇa [rite].
Whatever property of the Asuras there was, they gathered it together.
Indeed, it was their minds that they gathered together.
Deprived of mind, they were defeated.
One who has rivals should sacrifice [with this].
This, truly, is the grasping of minds.
He thereby grasps the minds of his rivals;
deprived of mind, they are defeated.
One who desires a village should sacrifice [with this].
This, truly, is the grasping of minds.
He thereby grasps the minds of his kinsmen;
grasped in mind, they do not depart from him.
They should bring clarified butter from the house of all the kinsmen.
From the houses of however many they bring clarified butter, the minds of all of them he grasps;
grasped in mind, they do not depart from him.
He offers with the āmana-mantras.
He makes them well-disposed.
'O divine Āmana, whichever kinsmen are like-minded, those I desire in my heart;
may they desire me in their heart;
make them well-disposed to me, svāhā!
O divine Āmana, whichever sons are like-minded, those I desire in my heart;
may they desire me in their heart;
make them well-disposed to me, svāhā!
O divine Āmana, whichever women are like-minded, those I desire in my heart;
may they desire me in their heart;
make them well-disposed to me, svāhā!
O divine Āmana, whichever cattle are like-minded, those I desire in my heart;
may they desire me in their heart;
make them well-disposed to me, svāhā!'
These are indeed the kinsmen: like kinsmen, like sons, like women, like cattle.
He unites himself with them;
he becomes [prosperous] through them.
A speckled milch cow is the dakṣiṇā,
for she belongs to the Viśve Devas.
Then, as to its being an offering to the Viśve Devas:
these creatures belong to the Viśve Devas;
he secures them;
he makes them [his] food.
The fore-offerings and after-offerings are outside the self;
the deity is the self.
If he were to offer either before or after the fore-offerings and after-offerings, he would place his kinsmen outside himself.
Then, in that he offers in the middle,
he places the kinsmen right within himself.
If he should desire: 'May they come quickly, may they depart quickly,' he should offer with a wooden [ladle];
for trees are moving and unmoving.
If he should desire: 'May they be firm, may they depart only with difficulty,' he should offer with an earthen [vessel];
for this [earth] is firm.
'Firm you are;
be firm among the gods;
may I be firm among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!
Fierce you are;
be fierce among the gods;
may I be fierce among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!
Overcoming you are;
be overcoming among the gods;
may I be overcoming among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!
Encompassing you are;
be encompassing among the gods;
may I be encompassing among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!
Wise you are;
be wise among the gods;
may I be wise among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!'
These are the kinsmen;
he establishes them in him;
he makes them non-departing from him.

2.3.3.m:
Now this horse is accepted [as a gift].
When accepted, being toothed on both jaws, it consumes his indriya-power and his cattle.
Varuṇa indeed is the horse, belonging to the deity Varuṇa.
Whoever accepts a horse falls into Varuṇa's power.
Therefore an offering of a horse-oblation is to be performed,
for being free from Varuṇa.
They are on four potsherds;
four-footed, indeed, is the horse;
by the potsherds he obtains it.
As many horses as there are, so many are the sacrificial cakes;
he frees him entirely.
There is one extra;
for that which he does not remember, for that is it.
He who is going to accept [a horse] again should not sacrifice [yet];
for if he were to accept again, he would fall into Varuṇa's power again.
Now, if he is going to accept again, half of his [cakes] should be to Varuṇa, half to Sūrya and Varuṇa.
In that [one cake] is to Varuṇa,
by that he frees him from Varuṇa.
Then in that [the other] is to Sūrya,
he receives it close to his own deity,
for the non-injury of himself.
There are twenty-one sāmidhenī verses;
yonder sun is the twenty-first;
yonder sun is Prajāpati;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
as great as the horse is, him he obtains.
All other animals have a womb, have a human womb;
the horse is womb-less, born of the waters.
In that this caru to Apāṃ Napāt is [offered],
he has given him a womb;
he establishes him in his own womb.
'Whatever noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, of gāyatrī meter, is established in the brāhmaṇa, that [noose] of yours I avert with this offering; to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
Whatever noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, of triṣṭubh meter, is established in the kṣatra, that [noose] of yours I avert with this offering; to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
Whatever noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, of jagatī meter, is established in the common people, that [noose] of yours I avert with this offering; to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
Whatever noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, of anuṣṭubh meter, is established in the quarters, that [noose] of yours I avert with this offering; to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!'
The meters are indeed the nooses of Varuṇa;
with them he seizes him;
with those by which he seizes him, with them he releases him.
He offers with two verses to Apāṃ Napāt;
from whatever Varuṇa is watery, from that he frees him.

2.3.4.m:
You are the life of Agni;
by that give life to him, to so-and-so.
You are the breath of Indra;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
You are the breath of the Fathers;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
You are the breath of the All-gods;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
You are the breath of Bṛhaspati;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
You are the breath of Prajāpati;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
What came as fresh became fresh butter; what crept became clarified butter;
what was sprinkled became ghee.
The path of ghee, the navel of immortality, given by Indra, offered by the Maruts;
that Viṣṇu saw, that Iḍā gavyā stirred up.
By the chant of the Pāvamāna, on the course of the Gāyatrī, by the power of the Upāṃśu [cup] I release you;
by the Bṛhat [and] the Rathantara, on the course of the Triṣṭubh, by the power of the Śukra [cup] I draw you up;
by the measure of Agni, on the course of the Jagatī, may god Savitṛ raise you up for life, for living!
This luster given by Agni has come, great bounty, strength, vigor, power;
for long life, lasting a hundred autumns, I receive [it], for great indriya-power.
Make this man, O Agni, fit for life, for luster, for sharp vigor, O Varuṇa, O King Soma!
Grant him protection like a mother, O Aditi, O All-gods, so that he may reach old age!
Agni is life;
his life-makers are men;
by that life be possessed of life!
Brahman is life;
its life-makers are brāhmaṇas;
by that life be possessed of life!
The sacrifice is life;
its life-makers are the sacrificial gifts;
by that life be possessed of life!
Immortality is life;
its life-makers are the gods;
by that life be possessed of life!
You are the breath of the Aśvins;
may they two give you breath;
live by that!
You are the breath of Mitra and Varuṇa;
may they two give you breath;
live by that!
You are the breath of Bṛhaspati;
may he give you breath;
live by that!
You are the breath of Prajāpati the highest;
may he give you breath;
live by that!

2.3.5.m:
He should offer a cake on eleven potsherds to Agni and Viṣṇu, a caru to Sarasvatī, [and] a caru to Bṛhaspati.
On the day before, he should make the diseased person sacrifice.
Agni, indeed, is all the deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
By the deities and by the sacrifice he places life in him.
Speech is Sarasvatī;
Brahman is Bṛhaspati.
By speech and by Brahman he places life in him.
Moreover, the deities are the breaths;
having seized the breaths on the day before, he fasts.
On the next day, he should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni, a caru in milk to Soma, a caru in ghee to Aditi, a caru made of barley to Varuṇa, [and] this [cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara;
he should make the diseased person sacrifice.
When someone dies, his body goes to Agni, [his] sap to Soma.
In that there is the [cake] to Agni,
he ransoms his body by that;
in that there is the [offering] to Soma,
[he ransoms his] sap by that;
having ransomed as much as he is—
Aditi, indeed, is this [earth];
upon her creatures are born;
upon her he brings him to birth.
There is a caru of this size;
such indeed is the self;
having freed as much as his self is from Varuṇa—
Agni Vaiśvānara, indeed, is the year;
he establishes him in the year;
he makes him possess the lifespan of a year.
[He says:] 'You are the life of Agni; by that give life to him, to so-and-so.'
Agni, indeed, is the giver of life to men;
he bestows life on him.
He offers with five [oblations];
fivefold is man;
as much as man is, he sets him in motion;
having set him in motion as much as man is—
This one indeed who purifies himself is breath;
he blows over from these quarters;
these quarters belong to these deities;
having set him in motion according to the deity from these quarters, he places the breaths in him.
'What came as fresh became fresh butter; what crept became clarified butter; what was sprinkled became ghee,' [he says];
he thereby proclaims the greatness of ghee;
also, he magnifies it.
'By the chant of the Pāvamāna, on the course of the Gāyatrī, by the power of the Upāṃśu [cup] I release you,' he says;
just as one releases, draws up, [and] raises one who is condemned to death, so is that.
As much as there are chants, cups, and meters,
by as much as there is, he places life in him;
by the sacrifice he places life in him.
All the priests recite around him;
all of them, indeed, heal this man;
all of them place life in him.
Taking hold of the Brahman priest's hand, they recite around him;
the Brahman priest is Brahman;
by Brahman alone he places Brahman-life in him.
They make him drink ghee off gold;
gold, indeed, is immortality;
ghee is life;
from immortality they make him drink life; he sucks it out, as it were;
he places life in himself.
He binds it upon him;
he makes him prosper with life.
With a verse to Agni he binds it on;
Agni, indeed, is all the deities;
by all the deities he places life in him.
'Agni is life; his life-makers are men; by that life be possessed of life,' [he says];
he who knows the gods as possessed of life and as life-makers reaches a full lifespan, [and] does not die before his full lifespan.
These deities, indeed, are possessed of life and life-makers, namely these breaths;
they place life in him.
'You are the breath of the Aśvins; may they two give you breath; live by that,' [he says];
these breaths, indeed, are those;
he places them in him;
he makes them non-departing from him.
Ten [cows] are to be given;
for ten are the breaths in the self;
he places the breaths in him.
A horse is to be given;
a garment is to be given;
gold is to be given;
an ox is to be given;
a boon is to be given;
much is to be given.

2.3.6.m:
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, [and a cake] on five potsherds to Indra.
Curd, honey, clarified butter, parched grain, [and] water: that is mixed together.
There is a caru for Aryaman.
The [cake] to Agni is for prosperity.
The [cake] to Indra is on five potsherds;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
fivefold are the offering-mantra and invitatory-mantra;
fivefold are cattle;
fivefold is man.
As great as man is, him he obtains;
having become complete, he obtains cattle.
For this mixture is like cattle;
moreover, as many cattle as there are, those he unites for him.
Regarding this they say:
"[The cake] to Indra should be made on eleven potsherds,
for cattle belong to Indra."
Moreover they say:
"It should be made for Prajāpati,
for cattle belong to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the progenitor of cattle;
him indeed he approaches with his share;
he propagates cattle for him."
Now this caru is for Aryaman.
He who gives is Aryaman;
giving is Aryaman;
subjects desiring to give come to be his.
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to brilliant Agni, a caru to Sūrya, [and a cake] on eight potsherds to brilliant Agni.
He should make one desiring sight sacrifice [it].
By the sight of Agni people see by night, by [the sight] of Sūrya by day.
These two indeed are the givers of sight;
these two indeed he approaches with their share;
these two bestow sight upon him.
"May he bestow equal sight."
Because the same deity is on both sides of the caru,
he establishes distinct eyes for him.
As much as he sees with one eye, so much he would see with both, if the caru were not in between.
Now because the caru is in between,
therefore this [space] is between the two eyes,
for separation.
White rice grains are [used]; they milk white cows for the clarified butter.
For yonder Āditya is like this,
for success.
It is in milk;
milk indeed is clarified butter;
milk is sight;
by milk indeed he places milk [and] sight in him.
He places [them] with [verses] to Sūrya;
he places sight in him.

2.5.1.m:
One desiring cattle should seize a brown, shaggy, tawny [animal] sacred to Soma.
Plants are sacred to Soma;
cattle are plants.
In that it is sacred to Soma,
he visibly seizes an animal for him.
It is shaggy;
this is indeed the form of prosperity;
he secures prosperity alone.
It is brown, tawny;
[it is] the form of Soma, for success.
One desiring cattle should seize one born last of triplets [sacred] to Soma and Pūṣan.
Soma indeed is the depositor of semen;
Pūṣan is the progenitor of cattle.
Soma alone deposits semen for him;
Pūṣan begets cattle.
Two of these are born to a teat, the third to vigor;
vigor is indeed cattle;
by vigor alone, having obtained vigor, cattle, he secures [them].
Thrice indeed in a year this one gives birth alongside other cattle;
this is indeed the form of prosperity;
he secures prosperity alone.
Some creatures have a share, others have no share;
in that the sacrificial post is of udumbara-wood,
he makes both kinds share-holding.
Month by month this [tree] ripens inside, [different] from other trees;
this is indeed the form of prosperity;
he secures prosperity alone.
One desiring cattle should seize a hornless [animal] sacred to Prajāpati.
Cattle indeed belong to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the progenitor of cattle.
Him alone he approached with his portion;
he begets cattle for him.
Prajāpati is indeed the womb;
from the womb he is born.
This [animal] corresponds to the forms of all cattle:
like a man's, its beard;
like a horse's, its head;
like an ass's, its ears;
like a dog's, its hairs;
like a cow's, its forefeet;
like a sheep's, its hind [feet];
it is indeed a goat;
having obtained all the forms of cattle, he secures [them];
all forms of cattle attend upon him.
The libation is accompanied by [the verse] 'Hiraṇyagarbha';
the sāmidhenī-verses are those of Prajāpati;
the āprī-verses are those of Prajāpati.
Gold is to be given,
for the possession of seed.
A tārpya-garment is to be given,
for having the same origin.
An outer garment is to be given;
by that he obtains and secures the forms of the sacrifice.
With this indeed Upakeru prospered;
he prospers who sacrifices with this.
Twelvefold indeed he enclosed the enclosure;
there he gave twelve boons each time.
That twelve are given is the copy of that.
He should seize a white [animal] for Vāyu;
he should make one desiring prosperity sacrifice.
Vāyu indeed is the most vigorous, the swiftest of the gods;
he leads him to prosperity.
They say about that:
'An unsettled deity is capable of wiping away, capable of leading him to distress.'
Beyond that, that very [deity] leads him to prosperity.
He should seize a white [animal] for Vāyu with teams;
he should make one desiring a village sacrifice.
Vāyu indeed leads these creatures about, tethered through the nose, thus and thus.
In that it is for Vāyu,
Vāyu alone leads to him the clan tethered through the nose.
The offering and invitatory verses are accompanied by teams;
he maintains the village in him.
It is white,
the form of sacred lore, for success.
He should seize a white [animal] for Vāyu with teams;
he should make a sick man sacrifice.
Breath indeed is Vāyu;
for breath has departed from him, and therefore he is sick.
In that it is for Vāyu,
Vāyu alone brings breath to him.
The offering and invitatory verses are accompanied by teams;
he maintains breath in him.
It is white,
the form of sacred lore, for success.
He should seize a white [animal] for Vāyu with teams;
he should make one desiring cattle sacrifice.
Breath indeed is Vāyu;
cattle run toward breath when they sniff the wind in the rains.
In that it is for Vāyu,
Vāyu alone brings cattle to him.
The offering and invitatory verses are accompanied by teams;
he maintains cattle in him.
It is white,
the form of sacred lore, for success.

2.5.10.m:
Yonder sun was deprived of its splendors;
then all this became darkness.
Prajāpati saw these ten bulls;
moreover they say Indra saw them.
He seized them as sacred to Indra.
Having attained the powers and virilities, he placed [them] in him by means of them.
In that [bulls] with a white mark on the forehead are seized, by means of them he placed splendor in him from the front;
in that [bulls] are white-humped, from above by means of them;
in that [bulls] have white backs, from behind by means of them.
Then yonder sun became splendid on all sides.
One who desires splendor should seize these bulls sacred to Indra.
In that [bulls] with a white mark on the forehead are seized,
from the front by means of them he places splendor in him;
in that [bulls] are white-humped,
from above by means of them;
in that [bulls] have white backs,
from behind by means of them.
On all sides he makes him splendid;
he causes him to attain the measure of yonder sun.
He should seize a tenth [animal] sacred to Prajāpati in the twelfth month.
Twelve months are the year;
having attained the year, he wins it.
Nine are seized;
nine indeed are the vital breaths;
the vital breaths indeed are virility in man;
he places the vital breaths, virility, in him.
Ten are seized;
the Virāj has ten syllables;
having placed these powers and virilities in herself, the Virāj —
this [earth] is the Virāj;
in this very [earth] he is firmly established.
Homage to the greatness, to the eye, O father of the Maruts! Thus I sing to thee: / Offered, go by the paths traveled by the gods; be established among the plants with thy bodies!
This was the tie of the gods, the lord of waters, the bull of plants; / Pūṣan chose the drop of Soma; high was the rock when that was [so].
The drop fell down along the earth, along heaven, and along this womb and that which was prior; / pouring the drop that moves along the common womb, I follow the seven Hotṛs.
Father of the calves, lord of the cows not to be slain, and he is the father of great water-buckets; / calf, afterbirth, fresh milk, biestings, curd, whey, and clarified butter are his womb.
The cows chose thee for kingship; the Maruts of fine songs magnify thee; / seated upon the heights with the peaks of dominion, then, fierce one, distribute wealth to us!

2.6.3.m:
An eight-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Agni,
a barley mess sacred to Varuṇa,
a gavidhukā mess sacred to Rudra,
curd sacred to Indra.
A milch draft-cow is the sacrificial gift.
From the descent of the waters they fetch apāmārga plants.
Having ground them into groats, having gone away to the south, having placed a single firebrand on a natural cleft, he offers with a dipping spoon of palāśa wood:
'At the impulse of the god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, with the might of Indra, you are the slayer of the Rakṣases, svāhā!
Slain is the Rakṣas;
we have killed the Rakṣas.'
A boon is the sacrificial gift.
'Which gods sit in the east, led by Agni, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Which gods sit in the south, led by Yama, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Which gods sit in the west, led by the Maruts, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Which gods sit in the north, led by Mitra and Varuṇa, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Which gods sit above, having food, led by Soma, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Here I sweep together the Rakṣas.
O Agni, burn up the Rakṣas!
Burned up is the Rakṣas.
To Agni sitting in the east, svāhā!
To Yama sitting in the south, svāhā!
To the Maruts sitting in the west, svāhā!
To Mitra and Varuṇa sitting in the north, svāhā!
To Soma sitting above, having food, the slayer of Rakṣases, svāhā!'
A chariot drawn by five horses is the sacrificial gift.

2.6.4.m:
A mess of boiled rice for Anumati;
a mess of boiled rice for Rākā;
a mess of boiled rice for Sinīvālī;
a mess of boiled rice for Kuhū;
a twelve-pot-sherd [cake] for Dhātṛ.
A four-year-old cow is the sacrificial gift.
An eleven-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Agni and Viṣṇu;
a mess of boiled rice sacred to Indra and Viṣṇu;
a three-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Viṣṇu.
A dwarf is the sacrificial gift.
An eleven-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Soma and Pūṣan;
a mess of boiled rice sacred to Indra and Pūṣan;
a mess of boiled rice sacred to Pūṣan.
A black [animal] is the sacrificial gift.
A twelve-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni Vaiśvānara;
a barley mess sacred to Varuṇa.
Gold and a horse are the sacrificial gift.

2.6.5.m:
A mess of boiled rice sacred to Bṛhaspati at the house of the Brahman;
a white-backed [animal] is the sacrificial gift.
An eleven-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Indra at the house of the king;
a bull is the sacrificial gift.
A mess of boiled rice sacred to Aditi at the house of the chief queen;
a milch cow is the sacrificial gift.
A mess of boiled rice sacred to Nirṛti made of [grains] winnowed with nails at the house of the discarded wife;
a black-and-white hornless cow that kicks away is the sacrificial gift.
An eight-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Agni at the house of the army commander;
gold is the sacrificial gift.
A two-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to the Aśvins at the house of the charioteer;
two twin calves are the sacrificial gift.
An eight-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Savitṛ at the house of the distributor;
a white [animal] is the sacrificial gift.
A ten-pot-sherd barley [cake] sacred to Varuṇa at the house of the bard;
a brown, large-castrated [bull] is the sacrificial gift.
A seven-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to the Maruts at the house of the village-headman of the Vaiśya class;
a speckled four-year-old cow is the sacrificial gift.
A mess of boiled rice sacred to Pūṣan at the house of the tax collector;
a black [animal] is the sacrificial gift.
A three-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Viṣṇu at the house of the carpenter and chariot-maker;
all [tools] of metal are the sacrificial gift.
A gavidhukā mess sacred to Rudra at the house of the dice-master and the hunter;
a sword tied with hair is the sacrificial gift, or a spotted three-year-old [bull], or a halter with a horsehair cord.

2.6.9.m:
'You are the womb of dominion;
you are the caul of dominion;
you are the navel of dominion.'
'Agni the lord of the house has recognized [him];
Indra of ancient renown has recognized [him];
Mitra and Varuṇa of fixed vows have recognized [him];
Heaven and Earth, increasers of order, have recognized [him];
Pūṣan who knows all has recognized [him];
the goddess Aditi has recognized [him];
recognized is this so-and-so, descendant of so-and-so, son of so-and-so, in such-and-such a clan, for great dominion, for great lordship over the folk!'
'This is your king, O folk;
Soma is the king of us Brahmans!'
'You are Indra's bolt that slays Vṛtra;
with you may he slay Vṛtra!'
'You are repellers of enemies;
protect [him] in front;
protect [him] behind;
protect [him] across;
protect [him] along;
protect [him] above;
protect him from the quarters!'
'You are Mitra;
you are Varuṇa.'
'Golden-coloured at the dawning of the dawn, with iron pillars at the rising of the sun, / do you mount the chariot-seat, O Varuṇa and Mitra, and thence behold Aditi and Diti!'
